                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This expansion is mostly just more buildings, decisions, horde nation mechanics, and new diplomatic options like ability to call allies into wars. They also made it so that rebels fight eachother and that certain actions require magistrates to represent the need for bureaucracy. \n\nMy biggest gripe about this expansion is that this is basically when Paradox shifted away from universal systems and mechanics to explain the world to giving countries their own special mechanics and systems. You could argue that they first did this with the Holy Roman Empire mechanics, but I think most would agree that was a genuinely exceptional circumstance. Paradox games have a problem where big countries like the Ming Dynasty are always stable. They basically have to cheat to make these empires actually decline and fall as they historically did. Even to this day they have still never overcome this problem.\n",
